WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:12.240
The world we experience
isn't just given to us.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.600
The brain is actively generating
our experienced worlds

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.720
every moment of every day.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:34.400
If I'm sitting on the beach,
and I open my eyes,

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:37.440
I suddenly see
before me the sea, the waves,

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:39.040
I can feel the breeze on my face,

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.480
I can hear the seagulls
in the distance.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.920
It seems like there's this
objective world out there,

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.880
and it's just pouring itself
into my mind through the senses.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.720
Yet, I know that's not
what's going on.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.360
The signals that arrive
at our eyes and our ears,

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:57.520
the light waves that hit our retinas,

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.800
the pressure waves
that come into our eardrums,

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:02.880
they don't come with labels on them,
like "I'm from a seagull",

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.920
or "I'm from the sea",
or "I'm blue".

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.520
They're just ambiguous signals.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.000
The brain has to make sense
of all these sensory signals

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:14.680
and figure out where they came from
and what they mean.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.880
That's the process of perception.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:23.760
So perception isn't just a reading
out of the world around us,

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.200
it's always a creative act
of interpretation

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:31.760
in which the brain is utilising its
knowledge about the way the world is,

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:35.400
to make its best guess
of what causes the sensory signals,

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:38.080
and that - that's what we
consciously perceive.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:42.560
So the brain has what you might call
prior knowledge,

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:45.800
about the structure of the world
and about what's out there,

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.600
that allows it to interpret
the sensory signals that come in.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:52.520
And sometimes this prior knowledge

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.880
is built deep into the structure
of our brain,

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.080
and it's knowledge that
we're not aware of having.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:01.240
For example, that light tends always
to come from above.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.120
And that means that,
when we look at shadows,

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:04.840
our brain is using that knowledge

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:08.600
so that we interpret what
shadows mean in a particular way.

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:11.120
It's not so much
"I'll believe it when I see it",

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.560
but if your brain believes it,
then you will see it.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:17.760
Perhaps the real world

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:20.520
just isn't something
that's useful for us to see.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.120
We are evolved
biological organisms,

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.800
our brains have limited capacities,

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:30.360
we see the world as it's most useful
to us in order to survive.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:34.480
Colour is a very, very familiar
feature of our visual experience

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.840
that gives our lives texture
and beauty and meaning.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:40.880
Does colour exist in the world?

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:46.000
Well not really, no. There are just
light waves of different wavelengths,

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:47.840
and out of those wavelengths,

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.920
the brain generates an infinite
variety of different colours.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:56.040
And why does it do this? Well,
it allows the brain to keep track

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.280
of objects as lighting
conditions change.

00:02:59.800 --> 00:03:01.600
As the artist Cezanne once said,

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:05.640
"colour is the place where
the brain and the universe meet".

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:12.880
This process is going to be a little
bit different for each of us,

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.120
we're all going to experience
our own interpretations

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.000
of even the same sensory data.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:24.560
And this brings up this idea
of inner perceptual diversity.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:28.280
Now we're all familiar
with the idea of external diversity.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:30.880
We all recognise that
we're different heights,

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.720
different skin colours,
different shapes,

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.480
and we're less familiar
with the idea of inner diversity.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:41.440
One reason is that
you can't see my experience.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.000
If you look at the blue sky,
the blue that you experience

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:47.960
might not be the same
as the blue that I experience,

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:50.440
even though
we'll both call it blue.

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:56.240
There's one example
of perceptual diversity,

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:59.400
which became very well known
a few years ago.

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:03.680
It was this photo of a dress that
half the world saw as blue and black,

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:06.560
and half the world saw
as white and gold.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:10.920
These bits are blue,
and these bits are black.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:14.480
Definitely.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:16.520
The people that saw it
as blue and black

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.000
were so convinced that
what they saw was the way it is

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:20.920
that they couldn't understand

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.840
how other people
could see it differently.

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:26.160
And the same went for the people
that saw it to be white and gold.

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:28.040
Don't you see it?

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:31.960
Part of the reason was that it was
a very badly exposed photograph.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:33.720
So it was very ambiguous,

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:36.600
and the colour that it seemed to be
depended on the assumptions

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:40.840
your brain was making
about the ambient surrounding light.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:45.600
Okay, if we see that
so differently,

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:47.600
what about the rest
of our experience?

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:52.520
How can we assume that in other cases
we're experiencing the same thing?

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:54.560
The answer is we probably aren't.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:57.440
It can almost be used as a lever

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:59.720
to help people better communicate
with each other.

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:01.960
Because if you can demonstrate

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.720
that even something
as apparently straightforward

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.920
as opening your eyes
and looking at a photograph

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.840
can lead to such different
beliefs about what's going on,

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:13.360
then we can begin to recognise

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:15.840
that other people
may see things differently,

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:17.440
believe things differently.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:20.480
A lot of challenges in communicating
with each other

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:21.800
might be rooted in the fact

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:24.840
that our literal experience
of the same world is different.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:29.200
It's not that these things
don't exist - they exist -

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.600
but the way
in which they exist for me

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:35.680
is completely dependent
on my brain.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:39.920
We do all experience a shared world,
a shared reality,

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:46.200
but our way of experiencing it
is individual. It's unique.

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:47.800
And it's very precious
because of that.

